From 64846b54305a595e2b9d43a88113c83304aa7c08 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Ian Blenke <ian@blenke.com>
Date: Tue, 25 Aug 2026 14:21:52 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 09933/11362] Resolve provenance receipts against the
 artifact's own commit

Operator decision on the 52 red deterministic-replay tests recorded at 3f0af06cd7.
A receipt hashed the working-tree copy of a shared capability spec. Spec-first
discipline requires every later experiment to append to that same spec, so the
recorded hash was guaranteed to go stale. The receipt was red for a reason that
was never a provenance break, and it was red on nearly every artifact at once.

REQ-REPORT-6610 was already in the spec. This commit adopts it: 50 replay-tested
modules now route their file-hash primitive through carnot.provenance_receipts,
47 under python/carnot/ and 3 under python/carnot/pipeline/.

Measured on the 68 `result == replay` tests, in a git worktree with PYTHONPATH
verified by import before every reading:

  before   52 failed / 26 passed / 1 error
  after    13 failed / 65 passed / 1 error
  39 flipped green, none went red

The receipt is not vacuous, which was the whole risk of this decision. Four
provenance breaks were proved RED against a real adopted module (exp5355) and
GREEN again on restore:

  a dependency the module does not produce      -> key-set difference
  a dependency absent at the artifact commit    -> ReceiptResolutionError
  an unresolvable artifact commit               -> ReceiptResolutionError
  a recorded hash that is not the committed one -> value difference

What it deliberately stops reporting is a later unrelated edit to a shared file.
That is the change, stated plainly. The signal it drops was never a provenance
break, and an always-red guard is read by nobody.

The 13 remaining reds are NOT receipt failures, and are four distinct causes:

  3  a fabrication-gate stamp the module never writes (exp5342/5368/5383)
  8  content PARSED out of a shared high-churn file, not hashed -- the 7
     transition modules read research-roadmap.yaml and exp5414 reads
     ops/conductor-log.md. REQ-REPORT-6610-SCOPE says receipts only.
  1  live machine state in the artifact: exp5736 records cpu_count and
     disk_free_mb, which no replay can reproduce
  1  exp5749's own validator rejects its checked-in artifact

Causes 3 and 4 are not named in the escalation note; they are recorded here
because a reader counting reds after this change would otherwise be surprised.

Not adopted, each with a reason, in the spec table: the shared path_sha256 in
exp5415 (six importers, so no single RESULT_RELATIVE_PATH is correct);
pipeline/verifier_dose_scheduler_replay (every path it hashes is under results/,
where EVIDENCE-LIVE already mandates a working-tree read); nine modules with no
file-hash primitive; and every module without a replay test, since adoption
there cannot be measured while the repository-wide suite is red.

Cost paid to land, recorded rather than hidden: `ruff format` reflowed all 50
modules, which is why the diff is ~1900 lines rather than ~100. Those files were
already non-conforming at HEAD because conductor commits skip hooks, and
`ruff-format --check` is a pre-commit hook. The 68-test result is identical
before and after the reflow, and the semantic change per module is two lines.

Building on a teammate's REQ-REPORT-6610-EVIDENCE-LIVE (ceb53bb9b7): a receipt
over results/** reads the working tree, because pinning it would hide a later
gate stamp or a dropped corrigendum. Adoption is measured with that rule active
and it costs nothing on this corpus.

